  "title": "The arras contract decides what you bought",
  "source_article": "property-contracts.txt, buying-10-steps.txt, bank-guarantees.txt",
  "post_text": "Buying in Spain: the arras contract is where you commit. It sets the terms of the purchase, so make sure you understand it fully. Your legal representative will walk you through the details (having one is strongly recommended), but ask for a version with English terms too, so you know exactly what you're signing.\n\nIn many cases, the real estate agent will ask for a holding deposit before starting work on the arras contract, typically €2,000 to €6,000. This isn't a legal requirement, so if you're not comfortable paying it, look for an agent willing to work on your terms.\n\nThere are different types of arras contract, the most commonly used is the Arras Penitenciales. This allows either the buyer or seller to walk away from the deal, subject to a penalty. The standard setup: if the buyer pulls out, they lose their deposit; if the seller pulls out, they must repay double the deposit amount. \n\nA well-drafted arras contract should contain:\n- Full identification of buyer and seller\n- Complete property description (including cadastral reference)\n- Total purchase price\n- Deposit amount and payment method\n- Type of arras explicitly stated (Arras Penitenciales)\n- Deadline for signing the final deed (Escritura)\n- Property encumbrances and possession status (Nota Simple)\n- Distribution of transaction costs (who pays what, like the real estate agent fees)\n\nThe biggest risk in Arras Penitenciales contracts involves mortgage financing. If you need a mortgage include a suspensive condition (condición suspensiva) that makes the contract conditional on obtaining mortgage approval. Specify: \n\n- Minimum loan amount required\n- Deadline for obtaining bank approval\n- Required documentation proving denial\n- What happens to the deposit if financing fails\n\nThe deposit\nThe deposit is typically agreed at 10%, though for larger purchases it may drop to 5%. This money is often paid directly to the seller, but where possible, it's recommended to use a safeguarded account with a financial institution or let an attorney hold the funds.\n\nBuying a new build? Then you'll also want completion date clauses and the bank guarantee that protects staged payments included in the contract.\n\nEven if the Arras contract is not signed at a notary, it is legally binding, so make sure you understand the terms you accept before signing it.\n\n#SpainProperty #BuyingInSpain #MoveToSpain # ArrasPenitenciales\n\nOwning or planning to buy property in Spain? Bueno is trusted by homeowners from 25+ countries. getbueno.com",
