  "title": "Spain taxes your empty holiday home",
  "source_article": "irnr.txt, non-resident-property-tax.txt",
  "post_text": "Spain taxes your Spanish home even when nobody is in it. The charge is called imputed income, and it is the bill most foreign owners never see coming.\n\nThe base is the cadastral value on your IBI receipt. Not market value, and not the price you paid. Getting this wrong overstates the tax several times over. For 2023, 2024 and 2025 the rate is 1.1 percent where your cadastral values were revised from 1 January 2012 onwards, and 2 percent where they were not. The rule for 2026 is not settled yet, and the 2026 filing window does not open until April 2027.\n\nThen the tax rate goes on top. 19 percent if you live in the EU or EEA and 24 percent for everyone else. The UK now sits in that second group.\n\nOn a cadastral value of 300,000 euros, at 1.1 percent, an owner in Norway pays 19 percent of 3,300 euros, about 627 a year. Cadastral values sit well below market, so take the figure off your IBI receipt, not off what you paid. No expenses of any kind come off imputed income. There is nothing to deduct.\n\nModelo 210 for a year up to 2025 can be filed any time in the following year, to 31 December, but direct debit closes on 23 December. From the 2026 year the window opens on 1 April.\n\nRental income is no longer quarterly. Rent for 2025 was filed in the 1 to 20 January window. From the 2026 rental year it moves again, to 1 to 20 April. One quarterly return is left: Q3 2026, due 1 to 20 October 2026.\n\nSpain treats you as a tax resident if you spend more than 183 days there, if your main economic interests are there, or, unless you can show otherwise, your spouse and dependent children live there.\n\nFile late of your own accord and there is a surcharge of 1 to 12 percent depending on how late you are, or 15 percent plus interest beyond a year. Wait for them to find you instead and the fine starts at 50 percent of the tax owed, a figure we state with the caveat that the underlying article has not been read verbatim. In extreme cases the consequences run to fines, closed bank accounts and confiscation of property.\n\nOne form, one figure off a bill you already receive. That is the whole job.\n\nOwning or planning to buy property in Spain? Bueno is trusted by homeowners from 25+ countries. getbueno.com",
