  "title": "Cash in Spain now needs paperwork",
  "source_article": "cash-rules.txt",
  "post_text": "Cash in Spain now comes with paperwork. If you are used to settling things in notes, the thresholds are worth knowing before you stand at a counter.\n\nSince 2025, withdrawals above 3,000 euros, or any amount involving 500 euro notes, have to be reported to the Agencia Tributaria 24 hours in advance. From 100,000 euros the notice period stretches to 72 hours. The bank does not do this for you.\n\nPayment limits sit separately. 1,000 euros for any transaction involving a business, 1,000 between private individuals, and 10,000 if you are a non-resident who does not pay tax in Spain.\n\nAny cash transfer within Spain over 100,000 euros, property purchases included, has to be declared on the S1 form. Sending more than 10,000 euros to someone means documenting where the money came from. You can hold up to 100,000 euros in cash inside Spain without declaring it.\n\nGet it wrong and fines climb in bands, up to 50 percent of the amount for minor infractions, 50 to 100 percent for serious ones, and 100 to 150 percent where fraud is involved. If payer or payee self-reports within three months, they may be exempt.\n\nCrossing the border with more than 10,000 euros in cash means declaring it, and travellers who do not risk losing up to half the undeclared amount.\n\nKeep receipts for large transactions for at least five years. The paperwork is the easy part. It is the not knowing that costs people.\n\nOwning or planning to buy property in Spain? Bueno is trusted by homeowners from 25+ countries. getbueno.com",
