  "title": "Tourist rentals need a licence",
  "source_article": "legally-renting.txt",
  "post_text": "What makes you a tourist landlord is not the length of the let. It is letting the whole furnished home, ready to move into, marketed through tourist channels, under your region's tourism rules. Duration is the wrong question, and most guidance asks it anyway.\n\nA genuine seasonal let under the general tenancy law is a different animal and sits outside the tourist regime entirely. So the same flat can be a tourist rental one month and an ordinary tenancy the next, depending on how it is let and how it is advertised.\n\nThe licence is regional. In the Comunitat Valenciana you need a favourable municipal report of urbanistic compatibility before registration. The registration lasts five years and renews with a fresh declaration and an updated municipal report. The registration number has to appear in all your advertising, and leaving it off is a serious infraction. Letting room by room inside a registered tourist flat is a very serious one.\n\nIn Andalucia, check the community's title deed and statutes first. A prohibition on tourist use written in there blocks the activity outright, whatever the region allows.\n\nIn the Illes Balears new tourist places are rationed through periodic calls rather than freely available. Since Ley 1/2025 it is a serious infraction to leave a party prohibition out of the contract, or to fail to require a guest causing a disturbance to leave within 24 hours.\n\nOn penalties, in the Balearics marketing tourist stays without authorisation is a serious infraction, 4,001 to 40,000 euros, and a very serious one runs to 400,000. Other regions set their own, and we do not have confirmed figures for them, so we will not publish a number we cannot stand behind.\n\nIn Murcia entry is by declaracion responsable. The procedure is continuously open and the activity may begin on submission, with a provisional number.\n\nOne region, one set of rules, one file. Start with the town hall and the community statutes, not with the listing.\n\nOwning or planning to buy property in Spain? Bueno is trusted by homeowners from 25+ countries. getbueno.com",
