  "title": "Fined for the advert itself: the EPC",
  "source_article": "epc.txt, lost-documents.txt",
  "post_text": "The energy certificate is part of the paperwork of selling or letting, and the rating on it decides how long it lasts. It lasts ten years, unless the rating is G, in which case five. A lot of older coastal stock rates G, so check the letter before you count on the date.\n\nThe certificate is required for new buildings and for existing buildings or parts of them sold or let to a new tenant. It has to be annexed to the contract, along with the label, and registered with your regional authority. Renewing with the same tenant does not itself trigger the obligation.\n\nThe scale runs A to G, and it is the letter that matters, not the number behind it.\n\nOn penalties, breaches are sanctioned under the state building law and enforced regionally. We do not have confirmed penalty figures, so check with your region before assuming a number. Plenty of ranges circulate online. None of them traces back to a source we could read, so we will not repeat them.\n\nThe EPC is required at the advertising stage, not just at completion. The certificate must exist before the property is listed. Expect 100 to 300 euros and less than a week. If yours has expired, a licensed assessor can reissue one for 60 to 150 euros.\n\nCheck the letter, check the date, then list.\n\nOwning or planning to buy property in Spain? Bueno is trusted by homeowners from 25+ countries. getbueno.com",
